Iron Deficiency Anaemia
Haematology and Oncology
Progressive fatigue/pallor, Pica (pagophagia), Koilonychia (spoon nails), Microcytic anaemia with low serum ferritin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-028
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of crushing systemic fatigue and syncope upon standing requiring urgent evaluation for severe symptomatic anaemia and active haemorrhage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who collapsed twice today when attempting to stand, complaining of a racing heart and complete physical exhaustion. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to assess for critical cardiovascular compromise and orthostatic shock secondary to profound anaemia, which posed a high risk for a fatal fall or ischemic event overnight.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: They state they have absolutely no energy left, their heart pounds out of their chest when they stand, and they keep passing out.
History of Present Illness: The 42-year-old patient reports an insidious 6-month history of progressive, generalized fatigue and severely reduced exercise tolerance. Over the past 48 hours, they experienced a sharp clinical decline, culminating in exertional dyspnoea and two episodes of orthostatic syncope today. They exhibit marked pica, specifically an unusual craving for non-nutritive ice (pagophagia), consuming several cups a day. They endorse a long-standing history of severe, heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) lasting 10 days per cycle. They report severe restless legs syndrome disrupting their sleep.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to orthostatic syncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of a traumatic head strike during further syncopal episodes.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 (Supine) -> 85/50 (Standing - Positive Orthostatic Drop) | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97 percent | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Pale, exhausted adult, chewing on crushed ice during the interview.
Physical Exam:
HEENT: Extreme pallor of the conjunctivae and oral mucous membranes. A painful, smooth, red tongue (glossitis) and angular cheilitis are present at the corners of the mouth.
Extremities: Marked pallor of the palmar creases. Fingernails demonstrate classic koilonychia (spoon-shaped, brittle nails). Capillary refill is 3 seconds.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, hyperdynamic circulation. A loud systolic flow murmur is auscultated across the precordium.
Neurological: Grossly intact. No focal sensory or motor deficits.
Environmental Observations: Large cups of ice beside the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter multivitamin with negligible iron content.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Iron Deficiency Anaemia - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Orthostatic Hypotension)
Differentials:

Acute Gastrointestinal Haemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the profound chronicity of the symptoms, presence of structural tissue changes (koilonychia, glossitis), and negative history of melaena or haematemesis.

Vitamin B12 Deficiency - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the specific presence of koilonychia and severe pica, alongside a clear history of chronic occult blood loss (menorrhagia) rather than malabsorption.

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the profound orthostatic hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and presence of a flow murmur, which indicate a severe oxygen-carrying deficit rather than pure metabolic slowing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe symptomatic anaemia, evidenced by profound orthostatic hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and a haemodynamic flow murmur. The physical stigmata (koilonychia, glossitis, pallor) and the behavioral symptom of pagophagia strongly confirm a chronic, absolute depletion of total body iron stores driven by prolonged menorrhagia. Delaying assessment until daytime hours would leave this patient vulnerable to further syncopal falls, resulting in severe trauma, or acute myocardial ischemia due to oxygen supply-demand mismatch. The urgent home visit was essential to physically evaluate the cardiovascular stability, prevent impending trauma, and arrange urgent phlebotomy and likely intravenous iron repletion.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed strict bed rest. Arranged urgent early-morning outpatient pathology for a full blood count (anticipating microcytic, hypochromic anaemia) and iron studies (anticipating critically low serum ferritin). Scheduled an urgent outpatient GP review to organize an intravenous iron infusion.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Transition from lying to sitting incredibly slowly, waiting two full minutes before attempting to stand. By allowing the cardiovascular system time to compensate for gravity, you actively prevent the blood from draining from the brain and eliminate the immediate fainting trigger.

Keep a bedside commode or urinal nearby tonight. By avoiding the long walk to the bathroom, you dramatically reduce the physical exertion on the heart and lower the risk of collapse.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review via telehealth tomorrow afternoon to review blood results and book infusion.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if chest pain develops, if shortness of breath occurs while lying entirely flat, or if a fainting episode occurs resulting in a fall that cannot be recovered from.

